# Namly Backend Architecture Rules

## Project Context

Namly is a backend-first food continuity platform for Colombian adults.

The system is built for:
- Strict multi-tenant data isolation
- High maintainability
- Modular domain separation
- Low-friction UX
- Scalable MVP architecture

---

## Tech Stack (Authoritative)

- NestJS
- TypeScript (strict mode)
- PostgreSQL (via Prisma ORM)
- Prisma ORM (PRIMARY database access layer)
- Supabase (Auth + Object Storage ONLY)
- EventEmitter2
- class-validator / class-transformer

---

# Core Architecture Principles

- Strict modular architecture (NestJS modules per domain)
- Clean separation of concerns (presentation / application / domain / infrastructure)
- Prefer composition over duplication
- Keep services small and focused
- Avoid overengineering and unnecessary abstractions
- Reuse existing abstractions before creating new ones

---

# Mandatory System Architecture

## Persistence Layer Rule (CRITICAL)

- Prisma is the ONLY ORM for PostgreSQL access
- All database access MUST go through Prisma repositories
- Supabase MUST NOT be used for database queries

### Supabase is restricted to:
- Authentication (JWT validation only)
- Object Storage (file uploads / downloads)

---

## Required Module Structure

Every module MUST follow:

modules/<module-name>/

- application/
  - services/
  - use-cases/

- domain/
  - entities/
  - interfaces/
  - rules/

- infrastructure/
  - repositories/

- presentation/
  - controllers/
  - dto/

- <module>.module.ts

---

## Layer Responsibilities

### Controllers
- Handle HTTP only
- Validate DTOs
- Call application layer
- No business logic
- No direct Prisma access

---

### Application Layer
- Orchestrates business logic
- Coordinates repositories and events
- Contains use cases
- No database implementation details

---

### Domain Layer
- Pure business rules
- Entities and interfaces only
- No framework dependencies

---

### Infrastructure Layer
- Implements repositories using Prisma
- Encapsulates database queries
- Never exposes Prisma client directly to upper layers
- Enforces multi-tenant filtering (profileId)

---

## Multi-Tenant Rule (CRITICAL)

- Every user-owned query MUST be scoped by profileId
- profileId MUST come from RequestContext
- No query may bypass tenant isolation

---

## DTO Rules

- Every endpoint MUST use DTOs
- class-validator is mandatory
- No raw request bodies allowed
- DTOs belong in presentation layer only

---

## TypeScript Rules

- Strict mode enabled
- No `any`
- Explicit return types for public methods
- Prefer readonly structures where possible

---

## Event System Rules

Use EventEmitter for:
- streak updates
- async computations
- notifications
- decoupled workflows

Avoid synchronous cross-module coupling

---

## Logging Rules

- Use NestJS Logger only
- No console.log in production code

---

## Reusability Rules

Before creating new code:
- check existing abstractions
- avoid duplication of repositories, DTOs, or logic
- extend or compose existing components instead of duplicating

---

## Naming Conventions

- kebab-case for files
- PascalCase for classes
- camelCase for methods

---

## Validation Rules

- ValidationPipe is global
- DTO validation must be declarative
- No manual validation inside services

---

## Security Rules

- Never expose Supabase raw user object externally
- Always map auth user → internal CurrentUser model
- All access must be tenant-aware via profileId

